steps.reasoning_content:
Let me look at the `starcos_gen_key` function more carefully. The vulnerability description says:

"The starcos_gen_key() function does not check the SW2 value when SW1 is 0x90. As a result, errors are not propagated and key generation proceeds with unallocated values."

Looking at the `starcos_gen_key` function (lines 1500-1549):

```c
static int starcos_gen_key(sc_card_t *card, sc_starcos_gen_key_data *data)
{
	int	r;
	size_t	i, len = data->key_length >> 3;
	sc_apdu_t apdu;
	u8 rbuf[SC_MAX_APDU_BUFFER_SIZE];
	u8 sbuf[2], *p, *q;

	CHECK_NOT_SUPPORTED_V3_4(card);

	/* generate key */
	sc_format_apdu(card, &apdu, SC_APDU_CASE_3_SHORT, 0x46,  0x00,
			data->key_id);
	apdu.le      = 0;
	sbuf[0] = (u8)(data->key_length >> 8);
	sbuf[1] = (u8)(data->key_length);
	apdu.data    = sbuf;
	apdu.lc      = 2;
	apdu.datalen = 2;
	r = sc_transmit_apdu(card, &apdu);
	LOG_TEST_RET(card->ctx, r, "APDU transmit failed");
	if (apdu.sw1 != 0x90 || apdu.sw2 != 0x00)   // <-- Line 1521-1522
		return sc_check_sw(card, apdu.sw1, apdu.sw2);
	/* read public key via READ PUBLIC KEY */
	sc_format_apdu(card, &apdu, SC_APDU_CASE_4_SHORT, 0xf0,  0x9c, 0x00);
	sbuf[0]      = data->key_id;
	apdu.cla    |= 0x80;
	apdu.data    = sbuf;
	apdu.datalen = 1;
	apdu.lc      = 1;
	apdu.resp    = rbuf;
	apdu.resplen = sizeof(rbuf);
	apdu.le      = 256;
	r = sc_transmit_apdu(card, &apdu);
	LOG_TEST_RET(card->ctx, r, "APDU transmit failed");
	if (apdu.sw1 != 0x90 || apdu.sw2 != 0x00)   // <-- Line 1535-1536
		return sc_check_sw(card, apdu.sw1, apdu.sw2);
```

The function sends two APDU commands. After the first one, it correctly checks both SW1 and SW2. But after the second command, it only validates the status bytes without properly handling the response data that should contain the public key modulus. The code proceeds to copy data from rbuf without verifying the response actually contains valid key data. The first APDU checks both SW1 and SW2 (lines 1521-1522), but the second only checks SW1 (line 1535). Since SW2 could indicate an error even when SW1 is 0x90, the function might continue processing with corrupted or unallocated buffer data. This means after the failed second APDU, the code still executes the memory allocation and copy operations, potentially using uninitialized or invalid data.

To trigger this, I need to construct an APDU sequence where the second APDU returns SW1=0x90 with a non-zero SW2, causing the function to bypass the error check and proceed with garbage values in the modulus buffer.

